    "answer": {
      "short": "N28°E.\n\nCheck both sine-rule configurations. The shorter direct route is within the plane’s demonstrated capacity, so it is excluded.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\angle LAB&=55^\\circ-30^\\circ\\\\\\angle LAB&=25^\\circ\\\\\\frac{\\sin\\angle ABL}{400}&=\\frac{\\sin25^\\circ}{200}\\\\\\sin\\angle ABL&=2\\sin25^\\circ\\end{align*}$$\nLet $\\alpha=\\sin^{-1}(2\\sin25^\\circ)$. The possible angles at $B$ are $\\alpha$ and $180^\\circ-\\alpha$.\n\nFor $\\angle ABL=\\alpha$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\angle ALB&=180^\\circ-25^\\circ-\\alpha\\\\\\angle ALB&=155^\\circ-\\alpha\\\\AB&=\\frac{200\\sin(155^\\circ-\\alpha)}{\\sin25^\\circ}\\\\AB&\\approx469.40\\text{ km}\\end{align*}$$\nFor $\\angle ABL=180^\\circ-\\alpha$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\angle ALB&=180^\\circ-25^\\circ-(180^\\circ-\\alpha)\\\\\\angle ALB&=\\alpha-25^\\circ\\\\AB&=\\frac{200\\sin(\\alpha-25^\\circ)}{\\sin25^\\circ}\\\\AB&\\approx255.64\\text{ km}\\end{align*}$$\nThe plane can fly the 400 km leg to $L$, so its capacity is at least 400 km. The 255.64 km direct route would be within that capacity and contradicts the question. Hence use $\\angle ALB=155^\\circ-\\alpha$.\n\nThe bearing of $A$ from $L$ is $125^\\circ$. Thus\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{bearing of }B\\text{ from }L&=125^\\circ-(155^\\circ-\\alpha)\\\\\\text{bearing of }B\\text{ from }L&=\\alpha-30^\\circ\\\\\\text{bearing of }B\\text{ from }L&\\approx27.6973^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$\nThe compass bearing is N28°E, to the nearest degree.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
